LEAD = {
    "headline": "THE LEAGUE OF LEGENDS WORLD FINAL LOST 41 PERCENT OF ITS PEAK AUDIENCE ON SATURDAY AND ITS HOURS WATCHED BARELY MOVED, BECAUSE THE BROADCAST RAN ROUGHLY 18 HOURS LONGER, AND ONE CO-STREAMER'S PEAK WAS 40 PERCENT THE SIZE OF TWITCH'S ENTIRE PLATFORM PEAK",
    "deck": "Here is the clearest demonstration this year of why hours watched is not an audience.  Nearly three million fewer people watched the League of Legends final at the same time as last year.  The hours figure went nowhere, because the event simply stayed on air longer.  And the audience that did show up was not where the sponsor bought.",
    "stamps": [
        ("STRAFE ON ESPORTS CHARTS  15 NOV", "https://www.strafe.com/news/read/worlds-2026-viewership-drops-41-outside-china-but-bilibili-draws-millions/"),
        ("STREAMS CHARTS  13 NOV", "https://streamscharts.com/news/streamer-awards-2026-results"),
        ("TWITCH BLOG  13 NOV", "https://blog.twitch.tv/en/2026/11/13/twitchcon-san-diego-2026/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Bilibili Gaming beat Gen.G three games to one in the League of Legends world final in New York on <mark>Saturday 14 November</mark>.  Esports Charts logged a peak of <mark>3,981,986 people watching at the same moment</mark>, against <mark>6,752,585</mark> for last year's final.  That is a <mark>41 percent fall</mark>, and the lowest peak a Worlds final has recorded since 2020.  For scale inside this brief, the biggest thing we measured earlier this month was the World of Warcraft launch at 1,119,734 on Twitch, so the final was still about three and a half times that.  It was also beaten outright this year by the Mobile Legends M7 championship at roughly 5.68 million, which means anybody still calling Worlds the biggest esports event of the year is reading a 2025 fact sheet.",
        "Now the number that will be quoted instead, and the reason you should refuse it.  Hours watched came in at <mark>over 130 million</mark>, against about <mark>132.9 million</mark> last year.  Essentially flat.  Read alongside the peak, that looks like an audience that held steady.  It is not.  The 2026 broadcast ran roughly <mark>107 hours</mark> of airtime against under <mark>89 hours</mark> in 2025, so the event was about a fifth longer.  Hours watched is viewers multiplied by time, and the time went up while the viewers went down.  The flat hours figure is not evidence of a healthy year.  It is the arithmetic of a longer show.",
        "The number nobody is leading with is the one that tells you what you actually bought.  Average concurrent viewers across the whole tournament were about <mark>1.22 million</mark>.  So the peak was roughly three times the typical audience.  If your rate was set against the peak, you paid for a moment and received an average, and the gap between those two things is where most live sponsorship money quietly goes.",
        "Then the part that changes who you call next time.  Esports Charts puts YouTube Gaming's peak for the final at about <mark>1.75 million</mark> and Twitch's at <mark>1.44 million</mark>.  Against that, the English-language co-streamer Caedrel peaked on his own channel at about <mark>580,000</mark>, which is roughly <mark>40 percent the size of Twitch's entire platform peak</mark>.  Hoang Luan did 211,000 and Ishooooo 125,000.  One person with a webcam and a strong opinion delivered a sizeable fraction of what an entire platform delivered, and the rights holder did not sell you that.",
        "One thing to hold onto before you take any of this to a negotiation.  Esports Charts excludes streaming platforms inside mainland China, and Bilibili separately claims more than 20 million people watched its team win.  That figure is measured on a different basis and cannot be added to the international peak, so the honest version is that the Western audience fell 41 percent and the Chinese audience is unknown to us.  The commercial point survives either way, because the inventory you buy in London or New York is priced on the number that fell.",
    ],
    "numbers": [("3,981,986", "Worlds final peak, down 41 percent"), ("107 HOURS", "airtime, up from under 89"), ("1.22M", "average concurrents, a third of the peak")],
    "flagnote": "Esports Charts flags these as preliminary figures and we are reading them through strafe's write-up rather than from the tracker's own page, which would not load for us.  In the first 48 hours after the final, numbers attributed to the same tracker circulated between roughly 3.8 million and 3.98 million as the preliminary count was revised, so treat the peak as about 3.9 to 4.0 million rather than as the exact figure it looks like, and note that the 41 percent fall holds at every version of it.  The platform peaks do not sum to the tournament peak, because channels peak at different moments and other platforms and languages are in the total, so do not add them together.  The co-streamer figures are preliminary too.  Esports Charts excludes mainland Chinese platforms entirely.  The World of Warcraft and Mobile Legends comparisons are Esports Charts figures for different events on different platforms, so they establish rough scale rather than a like-for-like ranking.",
    "so_what": "Hours watched rises when a broadcast runs longer, adds channels or adds languages, and all three happen on purpose, which is why sellers lead with it.  Peak tells you the single best moment.  Average tells you the audience.  The sharper lesson from Saturday is the second one: the audience is spreading onto co-streams the rights holder has no claim on, so the official feed is a shrinking share of an event's own attention.  You can buy the whole final and still miss the man in a bedroom with 580,000 people watching him react to it.",
    "do_this": "Before you sign your next live sponsorship, ask the seller in writing for peak concurrents, average concurrents and total airtime for last year's event, and work out the hours figure yourself from the second two so you can see how much of any growth is just a longer show.  Then ask what the deal covers on co-streams, and if the answer is nothing, go and price the three biggest co-streamers directly before you agree to the main feed.",
}

